# HCB Desktop: caption overlay and quality dashboard design

Your working directory is empty, so this is a design spec with starter code. I haven't written any files yet. The chart colors come from a palette that was already checked for colorblind safety. I didn't run the validator again, and I haven't rendered anything. Change the hex values to your brand colors and re-check before shipping.

## 1. Window architecture

Use two separate windows. The overlay must stay small, reliable and quiet. The dashboard is a normal app window.

```ts
// main/windows.ts
export function createOverlay() {
  const win = new BrowserWindow({
    width: 900, height: 220, minWidth: 420, minHeight: 120,
    frame: false, transparent: true, resizable: true,
    alwaysOnTop: true, skipTaskbar: true, hasShadow: false,
    focusable: true,            // must stay focusable for keyboard users
    webPreferences: { preload: path.join(__dirname, 'preload.js'), contextIsolation: true },
  });
  win.setAlwaysOnTop(true, 'screen-saver');           // stays above full-screen call apps
  win.setVisibleOnAllWorkspaces(true, { visibleOnFullScreen: true });
  win.setContentProtection(false);                     // let users screenshot their captions
  return win;
}

export function createDashboard() {
  return new BrowserWindow({ width: 1100, height: 760, title: 'Caption quality' });
}
```

- **Saved state** (electron-store): the overlay's position and size, font size, contrast preset, background opacity and line count.
- **Global shortcuts:**
  - `⌘⇧P` pauses and resumes.
  - `⌘⇧=` / `⌘⇧-` changes font size.
  - `⌘⇧,` opens settings.

  These shortcuts work even when the call app has focus. Users shouldn't have to hunt for a 20px button during a call.

## 2. Caption overlay layout

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ● Live                              [⏸ Pause]  [Aa]  [⋮]     │  ← control bar (fades in on hover/focus)
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ▌Priya   …so the rollout moves to Thursday, assuming QA       │
│          signs off by Wednesday noon.                         │
│ ▌Marcus  Works for me. I'll update the                        │  ← interim words in secondary ink
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

```html
<main class="overlay" data-contrast="standard" style="--caption-size: 28px; --lines: 3">
  <header class="controls" role="toolbar" aria-label="Caption controls">
    <span class="status" data-state="live"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Live</span>
    <button class="ctl" id="pause" aria-pressed="false">
      <svg aria-hidden="true">…</svg><span>Pause</span>
    </button>
    <button class="ctl" id="settings" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="settings-panel">
      <span aria-hidden="true">Aa</span><span class="sr-only">Caption settings</span>
    </button>
  </header>

  <section class="captions" role="log" aria-label="Live captions">
    <div class="turn" data-speaker-slot="1">
      <span class="speaker"><span class="chip" aria-hidden="true"></span>Priya</span>
      <p class="text"><span class="final">…so the rollout moves to Thursday,</span>
                      <span class="interim">assuming QA</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <aside id="settings-panel" class="settings" hidden>…</aside>
</main>
```

### Caption text area
- Show 2 to 4 lines (default 3), anchored to the bottom. New text pushes old text up in steps of one whole line. Don't use smooth scrolling. Moving text is harder to read and can cause motion sickness. Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, turn off all transitions.
- **Interim vs. final:** interim (not yet confirmed) words use secondary ink at normal weight and become primary ink once confirmed. Don't use italics or strikethrough, and don't let words jump around. When a word gets revised, swap it in place.
- Line length: `max-width: 60ch` per turn, so a wide window doesn't produce lines that are hard to follow.
- Typography: system sans, `line-height: 1.35`, `letter-spacing: 0.01em`, weight 500. Light weights disappear on a translucent background.
- Keep about 10 minutes of scrollback. The user can scroll up, and auto-follow pauses until they press "Jump to live" or reach the bottom again.

### Speaker labels
- Show the label only when the speaker changes, not on every line. The label goes in a hanging-indent column so the text stays aligned:
  ```css
  .turn { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; column-gap: 0.75em; }
  ```
- Each speaker gets a colored chip (a 4px vertical bar). **The name text itself stays in primary or secondary ink, never the speaker's color**, because light hues don't have enough contrast to be read as text.
- Assign colors to each person in the order they first speak, and keep them fixed: slot 1 for the first speaker, slot 2 for the second, and so on. If someone drops out, nobody else's color changes. After the 4th speaker, chips go neutral gray. The name is always the identifier and color only helps.
- Unknown speaker: label it "Speaker 3" and give it a dashed chip, so diarization uncertainty is visible.

### Pause and resume
- The button shows an icon and a text label, and the label changes: "Pause" ↔ "Resume". `aria-pressed` reflects the state.
- **The paused state must be impossible to miss.** Replace the status pill with "⏸ Paused, not captioning" and add a 2px dashed border around the overlay. The captions stay visible but dimmed to 60%. A frozen overlay must never look like a silent room.
- On resume, insert a divider ("Resumed 14:32 · 3 min gap") so the user knows content was skipped.
- The status pill has other states too: Live / Paused / Reconnecting / No audio. Each has an icon and text, never just a colored dot.

### Settings panel (popover, 280px, anchored to the Aa button)

| Control | Type | Values |
|---|---|---|
| Font size | Slider + live preview text, with −/+ steppers | 18–56px, 2px steps, default 28 |
| Contrast | Radio cards with a sample of each | Standard · High (white on black) · Yellow on black · Black on white |
| Background opacity | Slider | 40–100% (disabled at 100% for the High preset) |
| Lines shown | Segmented control | 2 · 3 · 4 |
| Show interim words | Toggle | on |

Changes apply immediately with no Save button. Esc closes the panel and returns focus to Aa.

### Overlay styling tokens

```css
.overlay {
  --caption-size: 28px;
  font: 500 var(--caption-size)/1.35 system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  border-radius: 12px; padding: 12px 16px;
  background: rgb(var(--bg-rgb) / var(--bg-opacity, 0.85));
  color: var(--ink);
}
.overlay[data-contrast="standard"] { --bg-rgb: 26 26 25;  --ink: #f0efec; --ink-2: #c3c2b7; }
.overlay[data-contrast="high"]     { --bg-rgb: 0 0 0;     --ink: #ffffff; --ink-2: #d6d6d6; --bg-opacity: 1; }
.overlay[data-contrast="yellow"]   { --bg-rgb: 0 0 0;     --ink: #ffd60a; --ink-2: #e0c25a; --bg-opacity: 1; }
.overlay[data-contrast="light"]    { --bg-rgb: 252 252 251; --ink: #0b0b0b; --ink-2: #52514e; }

.final   { color: var(--ink); }
.interim { color: var(--ink-2); }

/* speaker chips: palette slots stepped for the dark surface */
[data-speaker-slot="1"] { --spk: #3987e5; }
[data-speaker-slot="2"] { --spk: #d95926; }
[data-speaker-slot="3"] { --spk: #199e70; }
[data-speaker-slot="4"] { --spk: #9085e9; }
.chip { inline-size: 4px; block-size: 1em; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--spk, #898781); }

.controls { opacity: 0; transition: opacity .15s; -webkit-app-region: drag; }
.overlay:hover .controls, .overlay:focus-within .controls, .overlay[data-paused] .controls { opacity: 1; }
.controls button { -webkit-app-region: no-drag; min-block-size: 32px; }
:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ink); outline-offset: 2px; }

@media (forced-colors: active) { .chip { background: CanvasText; } .overlay { border: 1px solid CanvasText; } }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { transition: none !important; } }
```

Slot 4 uses violet instead of yellow on purpose. Yellow next to orange is the palette's weakest pair for colorblind users.

## 3. Quality dashboard (last hour)

### A metric problem to settle first
**Word error rate (WER) needs a reference transcript.** A live system has no ground truth. Choose your source before building the chart:
- **(a) User corrections:** edits divided by words.
- **(b) A second, slower high-accuracy model** used as the reference.
- **(c) Recorded test calls** scored offline.

Label the chart with whichever you choose, for example "Estimated WER (vs. reference model)". If you skip this, the chart will look more authoritative than it is.

### Layout

```
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Caption quality            [Last 15 min | Last hour ✓]  [Chart|Table] │  ← one filter row
├──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────────────┤
│ Median latency│ p95 latency  │ Est. WER     │ Words captioned      │  ← stat tiles (optional)
│ 0.82 s        │ 1.9 s ⚠      │ 7.4%         │ 6,412                │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Caption latency (seconds, per minute)                              │
│  line = median, light band = median–p95, hairline at 2s target     │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Estimated word error rate (%, per 5 minutes)                       │
│  12 columns, hairline at 10% target                                │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Table: 5-min buckets: Time · Words · Median · p95 · WER · Status   │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Why the charts look like this
- **Two charts, never one dual-axis chart.** Seconds and percentages on two y-scales let anyone make them appear correlated. Stack the charts with **aligned x-axes** (same width, same 0–60 min domain) so the eye can still compare them vertically. The crosshair is synced: hovering one chart draws the guide on both.
- **Latency:** a line chart per minute (60 points). The median is a 2px line in `--series-1` blue. The p50–p95 band is the same hue as a roughly 10% wash. That's still one hue and one story, so no legend is needed and the title names it. Add a direct label at the line end only ("0.8 s now").
- **WER:** columns per 5-minute bucket (12). Per-minute WER is too noisy at around 100 words. Columns are at most 24px wide, with a 4px rounded top and a square base at zero. Buckets with fewer than 50 words show a hollow outline and "low sample" in the tooltip.
- **Target lines** (2s latency, 10% WER) are 1px hairlines in muted gray with a text label at the right ("2 s target"). Use status colors only where a bucket breaches a target: a ⚠ icon plus text in the table and tiles. Never recolor the line itself.
- **Hover is on by default.** Latency shows a crosshair and a tooltip with time, median, p95 and word count. WER shows a tooltip per column with WER, words and errors. Hit targets are wider than the marks: each column hit area fills its whole 5-minute band.
- **Table view** is both the accessible version and the precise one. It uses tabular figures, right-aligned numbers, and a Status column with icon and text: "✓ OK", "⚠ Slow", "✕ Poor". The Chart/Table toggle switches the view in place. The table is also always rendered below the charts, because the user asked for both.

### Dashboard tokens

```css
.viz-root {
  color-scheme: light;
  --surface-1: #fcfcfb; --page: #f9f9f7;
  --text-primary: #0b0b0b; --text-secondary: #52514e; --text-muted: #898781;
  --grid: #e1e0d9; --axis: #c3c2b7;
  --series-1: #2a78d6;
  --status-good: #0ca30c; --status-warning: #fab219; --status-critical: #d03b3b;
}
:root[data-theme="dark"] .viz-root {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --surface-1: #1a1a19; --page: #0d0d0d;
  --text-primary: #f0efec; --text-secondary: #c3c2b7;
  --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #383835;
  --series-1: #3987e5;
}
/* also mirror the dark block under @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) */

.chart-card { background: var(--surface-1); border: 1px solid rgba(11,11,11,.1); border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px 20px; }
.chart-card h2 { font: 600 15px/1.3 system-ui; color: var(--text-primary); }
.line   { stroke: var(--series-1); stroke-width: 2; fill: none; stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: round; }
.band   { fill: var(--series-1); opacity: .10; }
.grid   { stroke: var(--grid); stroke-width: 1; }        /* solid, never dashed */
.target { stroke: var(--text-muted); stroke-width: 1; }
.tick   { fill: var(--text-muted); font: 12px system-ui; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
td.num  { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.stat-value { font: 600 28px/1.1 system-ui; }             /* proportional figures */
```

**Implementation:** use D3 scales with hand-built SVG, or Recharts if you're already on React. Either works with these tokens. Keep the data in the main process as a 60-minute ring buffer of per-minute aggregates. Send it to the dashboard over IPC every 5 seconds. Don't send raw events.

## Suggested file structure
```
src/main/{windows.ts, shortcuts.ts, settings-store.ts, metrics-buffer.ts}
src/preload.ts
src/renderer/overlay/{index.html, overlay.css, captions.ts, settings-panel.ts}
src/renderer/dashboard/{index.html, dashboard.css, latency-chart.ts, wer-chart.ts, table.ts}
```

If you tell me your stack (React or vanilla, and which chart library), I can scaffold these files. I'd start with the overlay, then render both windows to check label collisions and how the contrast presets look on a real call background.

